[2025-11-05 17:40:18] Datos recibidos: {"General information":{"UUID":"bef76a6a-d605-4653-ab01-2693e4df3722","Name":"Environmental impact of beef production in Mexico through life cycle assessment","Category":"Agricultura, Silvicultura, Pesca y Caza\/112 Ganader\u00eda","Description":"Intensive and extensive systems of beef production in the Mexican tropics were analyzed. The aim of the study was to estimate and compare the environmental impact of two typical beef production systems in Veracruz, Mexico. A system boundary was established from farm production to prior to transport for consumer consumption, including intermediate stages of product processing, marketing and transportation. The functional unit was 1 kg of boneless and fatless beef. The life cycle inventory was built using information from case studies; farmers, slaughterhouses and retail point managers who provided information from records and expert knowledge were interviewed. The study included cow-calf processes, pre-fattening, fattening, processing and retail. Several impact categories were analyzed using SimaPro software and the ReCiPe Midpoint method for impact characterization. The results showed that for the extensive system, the cow-calf stage is the major contributor in eleven of the twelve impact categories analyzed, while in the intensive system, it is the main contributor to the climate change, terrestrial acidification, freshwater and marine eutrophication. The fattening stage of intensive system, has a predominant impact on human toxicity, water depletion, terrestrial ecotoxicity, fossil depletion, photo-oxidants formation, freshwater ecotoxicity and marine ecotoxicity; the processing stage has relevant contributions to fossil fuel depletion and the formation of photochemical oxidants in intensive systems. Finally, the marketing stage did not have a predominant impact on any category. The results indicated that the extensive system has better environmental performance than the intensive system for nine of the twelve studied categories.","Tags":"Life cycle assessment of Mexican beef, Extensive and intensive beef production, Mexican beef supply chain, Environmental impact assessment of beef production, Beef production in the Mexican tropic","Technology | Description":"The analysis was performed using SimaPro 7.3.3 software for processing the inventory, ReCiPe Midpoint (H) method version 1.06. The ReCiPe method was selected because it is an actual and accepted method which uses mid and endpoint impact categories. It uses an environmental mechanism as a base for the impact model; similarly, it allows the quantification of the environmental impacts from specific indicators and was used to evaluate the twelve impact categories. These categories were: Climate change (CC in kg CO2 eq), Human Toxicity (HT in kg, 1.4-DB eq), Terrestrial Acidification (TA in kg, SO2 eq), Freshwater Eutrophication (FEU in kg Peq), Marine Eutrophication (MEU in kgNeq), Terrestrial Ecotoxicity (ET in kg 1,4-DB eq), Agricultural Land Occupation (ALO in m2 x yr), Water depletion (WD in m3), Fossil Depletion (FD in kg, oileq), Photochemical oxidant formation (POF in kg NMVOC), Freshwater Ecotoxicity (FE in kg 1,4-DB eq), and Marine Ecotoxicity (ME in kg 1.4-DB eq).","Code":"MX","Last_change":"05\/11\/2025 17:37"},"location":{"@type":"Location","@id":"3d26b0b1-7065-32cf-a9c0-6c010184c684","name":"Mexico","description":"reference location, sources: ISO 3166-1, ILCD","version":"00.00.000","code":"MX","latitude":23.951,"longitude":-102.535},"processDocumentation":{"LCI method":"Life cycle assessment (LCA) methodology according to ISO 14040:2006, using SimaPro 7.3.3 software and ReCiPe Midpoint (H) method version 1.06 for impact characterization","Process type":"Cradle-to-grave approach from farm production to prior to transport for consumer consumption, including intermediate stages of product processing, marketing and transportation","Modeling constants":"Tier 1 methodology of IPCC (2006) for methane emissions from cattle enteric fermentation and manure; direct and indirect nitrous oxide and ammonia manure emissions; infrastructure and equipment not considered according to LCA best practices; economic allocation (64.5% to milk and 35.5% to calf production) for cow-calf stage","Data source information":"17 case studies via interviews with farmers, slaughterhouse representatives and marketing point sellers; supplemented by literature values and expert interviews; data from FIRA (2015), INEGI (2015), ANETIF (2011), CEPAL-FAO-ICA (2013)","Data completeness":"Complete coverage of all relevant flows including animals, pasture, concentrates, fertilizers, pesticides, water, electricity, gas, packaging, materials for distribution, organic waste disposal and product transportation","Data selection":"Included all material and energy flows directly associated with beef production chain; excluded infrastructure and equipment as per LCA best practices","Data treatment":"Field data compared with published data, reports and domestic statistics; validated through consultations with livestock associations, researchers, academics and extension specialists","Sampling procedure":"Purposive sampling of 2 ranches for cow-calf process; 2 case studies per process and system for pre-fattening, fattening and processing stages; convenience sampling for marketing points","Data collection period":"One year of operations for each livestock production, processing and marketing unit analyzed","Use advice":"Results specific to Mexican tropical conditions in Veracruz; applicable to similar dual-purpose and feedlot systems; limitations include regional specificity and sample size constraints","Completeness":"High completeness with comprehensive coverage of all major life cycle stages and environmental impact categories","Sources":"IPCC (2006), ISO (2006), European Commission (2010), FIRA (2015), INEGI (2015), ANETIF (2011), CEPAL-FAO-ICA (2013), FAO (2010), SIAP (2011)","Project":"Knowledge-based Sustainable vAlue-added food chains: innovative tools for monitoring ethical, environmental and Socio-economical impActs and implementing Eu-Latin America shared strategies (Grant agreement no.: 265927)","Intended application":"Environmental impact assessment of beef production systems in Mexico; identification of hotspots along beef value chains; recommendations for sustainable production improvements","Data set owner":"Universidad Nacional Aut\u00f3noma de M\u00e9xico, Facultad de Medicina Veterinaria y Zootecnia and Instituto de Ingenier\u00eda","Data set generator":"Adriana Rivera Huerta, Leonor Patricia G\u00fcereca, Mar\u00eda de la Salud Rubio Lozano","Data set documentor":"Adriana Rivera Huerta, Leonor Patricia G\u00fcereca, Mar\u00eda de la Salud Rubio Lozano","Publication":"Resources, Conservation and Recycling 109 (2016) 44-53","Creation date":"2016","Copyright":"\u00a9 2016 Published by Elsevier B.V.","Access and use restrictions":"Standard Elsevier copyright restrictions apply"},"actors":{"@id":"47a88430-1dd2-4569-9741-8249a6672ae2","name":"IINGEN-UNAM","description":"Instituto de Ingenier\u00eda de la UNAM","category":"Organizaciones de investigaci\u00f3n","version":"01.00.000","lastChange":"2025-11-05T17:38:20.611Z","address":"Circuito Escolar s\/n, Ciudad Universitaria","city":"Coyoac\u00e1n","zipCode":"04510","country":"MX","email":"APadillaR@iingen.unam.mx","telephone":"+52 5556233600","telefax":null,"website":"http:\/\/www.iingen.unam.mx"},"Inputs":[{"is_reference":false,"flow":"Forage consumption","category":"","amount":13.4,"unit":"kg DM\/head\/d","uncertainty":"","provider":"","description":"Forage consumption for cows in production"},{"is_reference":false,"flow":"Forage consumption","category":"","amount":8.5,"unit":"kg DM\/head\/d","uncertainty":"","provider":"","description":"Forage consumption for heifers"},{"is_reference":false,"flow":"Forage consumption","category":"","amount":15,"unit":"kg DM\/head\/d","uncertainty":"","pr