[2025-11-21 21:07:07] First 500 chars: {"General information":{"UUID":"0c097336-f77e-488f-a58c-c92a92f4924c","Name":"Environmental assessment of jalapeño pepper (Capsicum annuum L.) cultivation using the LCA methodology: Comparison between conventional open field and aeroponic production","Category":"Agricultura, Silvicultura, Pesca y Caza/111 Crop Production","Description":"Purpose The rising global demand for vegetables has expanded their production through both conventional and non-conventional systems, reinforcing their role in 
[2025-11-21 21:07:07] Last 100 chars: provider":"Chamas et al. 2020","description":"Microplastic emissions from LDPE mulch degradation"}]}
[2025-11-21 21:07:07] Datos recibidos: {"General information":{"UUID":"0c097336-f77e-488f-a58c-c92a92f4924c","Name":"Environmental assessment of jalape\u00f1o pepper (Capsicum annuum L.) cultivation using the LCA methodology: Comparison between conventional open field and aeroponic production","Category":"Agricultura, Silvicultura, Pesca y Caza\/111 Crop Production","Description":"Purpose The rising global demand for vegetables has expanded their production through both conventional and non-conventional systems, reinforcing their role in food security but also intensifying environmental pressures. Jalape\u00f1o pepper (Capsicum annuum L.), a crop of high economic relevance, provides a case study to assess these trade-offs. This study presents a comparative life cycle assessment between conventional open- field cultivation and aeroponic systems under controlled conditions. Methods A comparative life cycle assessment (LCA) was carried out in accordance with ISO 14040\/44 guidelines, using the ReCiPe 2016 methodology at midpoint and end point level. The functional unit was defined as 1 kg of fresh jalape\u00f1o pepper. Inventory data for conventional cultivation were obtained from technical field reports, while aeroponic data were collected directly from measurements in an experimental aeroponic chamber. The system boundaries included upstream processes related to raw material and energy inputs. Emissions to soil, air, and water were estimated using emission factors reported by various authors and methodologies, including IPCC guidelines, EMEP, and GREET. Results and discussion Aeroponics exhibited greater environmental burdens in most indicators, with a carbon footprint more than ten times higher (6.11 vs 0.56 kg CO2 eq.\/kg) and a fossil resource use fourteen times higher (1.90 vs 0.14 kg oil eq.\/kg). However, the lower aeroponic impact compared to conventional farming in land use (0.007 vs 0.054 m2xa\/kg) and freshwater eutrophication (0.00016 vs 0.00024 kg P eq.\/kg) suggest specific mitigation opportunities. Comparisons with other vegetable production systems show that aeroponics and greenhouses generally display elevated carbon footprints per kg, strongly influenced by energy dependence. In Mexico, this is exacerbated by an electricity matrix dominated by fossil fuels, which amplifies upstream burdens. These findings highlight that yield improvements, energy efficiency, and renewable integration are crucial to enhance environmental performance. Conclusions This study demonstrates that the sustainability of advanced agricultural systems such as aeroponics critically depends on agronomic efficiency and the energy profile of the electricity grid. In contexts where fossil fuels dominate power generation, aeroponics may shift environmental impacts upstream, limiting its overall competitiveness. These findings provide key insights for designing resilient, scalable, and environmentally viable non-conventional agricultural systems on a global scale.","Tags":"Life Cycle Assessment, Agricultural Systems, Environmental Impact, Sustainable Agriculture, Urban Farming","Technology | Description":"A comparative life cycle assessment (LCA) was carried out in accordance with ISO 14040\/44 guidelines, using the ReCiPe 2016 methodology at midpoint and end point level. The functional unit was defined as 1 kg of fresh jalape\u00f1o pepper. Inventory data for conventional cultivation were obtained from technical field reports, while aeroponic data were collected directly from measurements in an experimental aeroponic chamber. Emissions to soil, air, and water were estimated using emission factors reported by various authors and methodologies, including IPCC guidelines, EMEP, and GREET.","Code":"MX","Last_change":"21\/11\/2025 21:02"},"location":{"@type":"Location","@id":"3d26b0b1-7065-32cf-a9c0-6c010184c684","name":"Mexico","description":"reference location, sources: ISO 3166-1, ILCD","version":"00.00.000","code":"MX","latitude":23.951,"longitude":-102.535},"processDocumentation":{"LCI method":"Life cycle assessment conducted according to ISO 14040\/44 guidelines using ReCiPe 2016 methodology at midpoint and endpoint level","Process type":"Cradle to extended gate - includes upstream processes related to raw material and energy inputs, agricultural production modules, and transportation to distribution center for conventional system; excludes infrastructure and equipment production","Modeling constants":"Functional unit: 1 kg of fresh jalape\u00f1o pepper; System boundaries exclude infrastructure and equipment production; Emissions estimated using IPCC Tier 1 methodologies, EMEP Tier 1 factors, GREET model, and specific emission factors from literature","Data source information":"Conventional cultivation: FIRA technical field reports (2024); Aeroponic cultivation: direct measurements from experimental aeroponic chamber; Background processes: Ecoinvent database; Emission factors: IPCC (2019), EMEP (2023), GREET (2023), Hussain et al. (2021), EPA Pesticide Water Calculator v1.52","Data completeness":"Includes all major material and energy flows; excludes infrastructure and equipment production; microplastic emissions acknowledged but not included in impact assessment due to methodological limitations","Data selection":"Includes water, fertilizers, pesticides, agrochemicals, plastics, electricity, fuel; excludes infrastructure; transportation included for conventional system but excluded for aeroponic system as it's intended for direct urban consumption","Data treatment":"Emissions to soil, air and water estimated using standardized methodologies; microplastic degradation modeled using first-order degradation model with 22 um\/year conversion rate; pesticide emissions calculated using EPA Pesticide Water Calculator","Sampling procedure":"Conventional: technical field reports and material use records; Aeroponic: direct measurements and material records from experimental trial","Data collection period":"2024 for primary data collection; background data from Ecoinvent 2022","Use advice":"Results specific to Mexican context with high fossil fuel dependency in electricity grid; sensitivity to electricity mix demonstrated; aeroponic system assumes direct urban consumption without transportation","Completeness":"High completeness for foreground processes; acknowledges limitations in microplastic impact assessment due to lack of characterization factors in ReCiPe methodology","Sources":"FIRA (2024), IPCC (2019), EMEP (2023), GREET (2023), Hussain et al. (2021), EPA (2016), Chamas et al. (2020), Ecoinvent database, Llorach et al. (2017)","Project":"Comparative environmental assessment of jalape\u00f1o pepper cultivation systems","Intended application":"Academic research, comparison of conventional vs aeroponic agricultural systems, decision support for sustainable agriculture","Data set owner":"Authors of the manuscript","Data set generator":"C.Y.M.A., J.J.M.N., J.R.C.C., J.E.B.A.","Data set documentor":"C.Y.M.A., J.R.C.C., R.C.S.S., J.E.B.A., C.M.N., J.J.M.N.","Publication":"International Journal of Life Cycle Assessment - Manuscript JLCA-D-25-00618R1","Creation date":"2024","Copyright":"Not specified in provided text","Access and use restrictions":"Datasets available from corresponding author on reasonable request; no competing interests declared"},"actors":{"@id":"47a88430-1dd2-4569-9741-8249a6672ae2","name":"IINGEN-UNAM","description":"Instituto de Ingenier\u00eda de la UNAM","category":"Organizaciones de investigaci\u00f3n","version":"01.00.000","lastChange":"2025-11-21T21:03:56.386Z","address":"Circuito Escolar s\/n, Ciudad Universitaria","city":"Coyoac\u00e1n","zipCode":"04510","country":"MX","email":"APadillaR@iingen.unam.mx","telephone":"+52 5556233600","telefax":null,"website":"http:\/\/www.iingen.unam.mx"},"Inputs":[{"is_reference":false,"flow":"Urea 46-00-00","category":"C:Manufacturing\/20:Manufacture of chemicals and chemical products\/201:Manufacture of basic chem